# mtab AND utmp ARE RUNTIME STATE, SO THESE TWO ARE CREATE-ONLY.  A fresh disk
# needs them present and empty; a running system must keep its own.  mkimage
# builds with ROOT=/v10, where the copy is right, but ipnxbuild builds with
# ROOT= -- the machine you are standing on -- and an unconditional copy there
# truncates the live mount table.  `df' then answers `/usr: can't find
# filesystem' on a machine whose /usr is mounted, because df reads mtab and
# mount.c only writes it on a successful mount.  Same for utmp and who(1).
$ETC/mtab: $PREP $GEN/etc/mtab
