# install target.
# flex BOOTSTRAPS FROM initscan.c, AND ITS PORT SWITCH IS -DSYS_V.
# Makefile:71-72 is `scan.c : scan.l' / `$(FLEX) -ist $(FLEX_FLAGS) scan.l
# >scan.c' with FLEX=./flex (:28) -- a flex is needed to build flex, and the
# redirect makes an EMPTY scan.c when there is none, which cc compiles without
# complaint into an empty object.  That, not a missing source, is
# `Undefined: _flexscan _yyin'.  Makefile:18 says what to do -- "the first
# time around use `make first_flex'" -- and :62-64 is `cp initscan.c scan.c'
# then make; the copy is done here so CFLAGS reaches the compile.
#
# -DSYS_V IS THE PORT FLAG, and Makefile:7 names it: "For System V Unix
# machines, add -DSYS_V to CFLAGS".  SYS_V occurs exactly twice in flex, both
# in flexdef.h: :33 takes <string.h>, defines bzero as memset and declares
# `char *memset()', while :59 is `#ifndef SYS_V' over <strings.h>.  v10 has
# string.h and memset.o and has neither strings.h nor bzero, bcopy or bcmp --
# none of the three is in libc.a -- so SYS_V is the branch that fits, and it
