# u9fs HAS BOTH A makefile AND AN mkfile, AND ONLY THE mkfile IS V10'S.
# u9fs.c:16-36 configures itself from one of three defines: SYSV takes
# <dirent.h> and turns SOCKETS on, BSD takes <sys/dir.h> and turns SOCKETS on,
# and V10 takes <ndir.h> and leaves it OFF.  The makefile is the generic
# distribution one -- `-DSYSV' and a link against -lbsd, a System V host's
# Berkeley compatibility library -- and there is no libbsd on these tapes;
# the mkfile beside it is `-DV10' with no -l at all.  make reads makefile
# before Makefile (main.c:219-220) and never looks at an mkfile, which is why
# this asked for sockets it was never meant to need.
#
# The V10 branch is the whole answer: /usr/include/ndir.h declares struct
# direct, DIR, opendir, readdir, telldir, seekdir and closedir, and libc.a
# carries opendir.o, readdir.o, closedir.o, seekdir.o, telldir.o, dirread.o
# and pdirread.o.  The u9fs binary the tape ships proves it -- its symbol
# table holds _opendir _readdir _closedir _seekdir _dirread _pdirread and not
# one socket symbol.  It takes its connection on stdin: README:1-3, "run on a
# Unix machine with its standard input, output, and error connected to a
# network connection".
P_CMD_u9fs_u9fs:V:	$PREP $LCCOK
	cd $CMD/u9fs; mk u9fs || echo 'u9fs: FAILED'

$CMD/u9fs/u9fs: P_CMD_u9fs_u9fs
